Vercel
We have discovered a security incident that involved unauthorized
access to certain internal Vercel systems. We are actively
investigating and have engaged incident response experts to help
with investigation and remediation.

Initially, we identified a limited subset of customers whose
Vercel credentials were compromised. We reached out to that
subset and recommended that they rotate their credentials
immediately.

At this time, we do not have reason to believe that your Vercel
credentials or personal data have been compromised.

We continue to investigate whether any data was exfiltrated and
will contact customers if we discover further evidence of
compromise. We've deployed extensive protective measures and
monitoring, and our services remain operational.

We recommend that all Vercel customers follow the Security
Bulletin
(https://vercel.com/kb/bulletin/vercel-april-2026-security-incident)
closely, which we will update regularly. Here are some best
practices you should follow:

*
Review the activity log (https://vercel.com/activity-log) for any
suspicious account activity
*
Review and rotate environment variables
(http://vercel.com/all-env-vars) used in your projects
*
Take advantage of the sensitive environment variables
(https://vercel.com/docs/environment-variables/sensitive-environment-variab=
les)
feature

-----------------------------------------------------------------

If you'd like to report an issue, reach out to Vercel Help
(https://vercel.com/help?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dnotifications).

Manage your notification settings
(https://vercel.com/d?to=3D%2F%5Bteam%5D%2F%7E%2Fsettings%2Fnotifications&t=
itle=3DNotification+Settings)

Copyright =C2=A9 2026 Vercel Inc.
(https://vercel.com?utm_medium=3Demail&utm_source=3Dnotifications)
